The historical manuscript already contained several seeds of the present theory: a complete
set of sixteen binary CMs, matrix transformations of logical operators, same-operands operator
superposition, formula-valued LMs, logical pairing, support-style “quotient” operations, single-
variable factorization, and tentative 4 × 4 constructions [10]. The present paper retains the sound
parts in a more explicit type system. In particular, the old arithmetic-modulo analogy is not
retained; its useful core is an ordinary Boolean support difference. The earlier higher-dimensional
language is replaced by a 2n-entry tensor with explicitly declared matrix flattenings.
The central structural claim is not that truth tables, Boolean matrices, tensor flattenings, or
pointwise composition are new. The distinctive focus is the formula-valued LM layer and the
compatibility of its semantics with the numeric CM layer. The architecture organizes several levels
of the same Boolean function:
1. formula-valued logical operators: for example [MX∨Y ], whose four entries are X ∨Y ,
X ∨¬Y , ¬X ∨Y , and ¬X ∨¬Y ;
