14
Discussion
14.1
What makes this a calculus rather than a list of identities
The formula-valued LM is the organizing symbolic object of the calculus. Boolean superposition
acts directly on aligned LMs; valuation sends the result to numeric correspondence tensors; signed
input-frame changes reindex the symbolic and numeric levels consistently; and logical pairing is
itself a Boolean homomorphism for the same pointwise operation. The raw cellwise truth-vector
law is classical, but the LM layer records which formulas and polarities each cell means, so the
frame condition becomes an explicit semantic type rather than an implicit ordering convention.
Theorem 6.12 collects these compatibility laws.
Two structural results explain why the pieces fit. The frame normal form [MXΘY ] = SX[Θ]SY
identifies the symbolic polarity matrix as an invertible change of Boolean frame, while the partition-
selector theorem explains why pairing preserves arbitrary outer Boolean operations. Ordinary
matrix multiplication then has its own matched-intermediate-frame law, separate from pointwise
superposition. Together these distinguish a typed calculus from a mere list of matrix-decorated
truth identities without claiming a new underlying Boolean algebra.
Higher-dimensional matrices then arise from variable grouping rather than from a new truth
domain. The order-n tensor is primary; a 2p × 2q matrix is one flattening. This removes ambiguity
from historical higher-dimensional descriptions and makes recursive block structure explicit.
14.2
Why the sixteen-element phase algebra is deferred
The cyclic phase algebra is mathematically interesting, but including its full development here
would change the paper’s question. The CM/LM calculus uses XOR–AND selection, formula
valuation, logical pairing, signed reindexing, and Boolean pointwise/outer composition. The phase
construction adds a second multiplication—cyclic convolution of coefficient positions—and with
it units, nilpotents, an augmentation filtration, and modal interpretations. Those are valuable
consequences of the four-position geometry, but they are not needed to define or prove the CM/LM
results above.
The clean paper boundary is therefore:
• this paper proves that literal CM rotation is a logical signed-frame action and develops the
CM/LM calculus independent of any phase multiplication;
• the phase paper may begin from that action, choose the cyclic coefficient order, introduce
convolution, and study the resulting algebra F2[C4] ∼= F2[u]/(u4) and its applications.
This separation lets the phase paper refer back to a fully developed logical foundation rather than
forcing the foundations paper to justify a new scalar interpretation.
14.3
Open structural questions
Several directions now become sharply formulable.
1. Clone and categorical formulation. The faithful preservation of Boolean superposition sug-
gests a clone-theoretic representation, while valuation and frame actions suggest an equivariant
or functorial formulation.
2. Minimal generating operations. Which subset of frame actions, pointwise lifts, outer lifts,
contractions, support differences, and substitutions generates the full finite CM/LM calculus?
25


3. Equivalence and canonical forms. Signed permutations give one natural orbit relation;
NPN equivalence adds output phase. Their relation to formula-level equivalence and efficient
canonicalization should be separated carefully.
4. Higher-dimensional block algorithms. The block-lift theorem gives a recursive construction
of large correspondence matrices. It remains an empirical question when a symbolic block
representation avoids materializing all 2n entries.
5. Rotation-derived algebra. Once cyclic convolution is intentionally added, the C4 algebra and
its higher-arity analogues raise separate questions involving group algebras, finite differences,
ANF degree, and modal resource theories.
6. Operator-valued enrichment. The present uppercase operands X, Y, A, B are arbitrary
Boolean formulas, but the LM entries still live in one Boolean formula algebra. A separate
extension could ask when formula slots may themselves carry matrix- or operator-valued objects
while preserving frame normalization, pairing, and superposition.
